KG42.register("source_edge_archive-9c86c8406e0d4e78e7e8d94b",{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R003::E::v141-c-R003-E001","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","rule_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R003","record":{"edge_id":"v141-c-R003-E001","source":"coordination.price_signal","target":"clearing.social_welfare","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R003-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e14","v141-b-g03-e11","v141-b-g03-e15"]},"evidence_class":"S","provenance_gate":"direct_C_edge_with_constraint_and_source_excerpt","scientific_acceptance":false,"accepted_constraint_ids":["v141-c-R003-C001"],"held_constraint_ids":[],"semantic_review_status":"supported","source":"COMP-166b347ab5fcd2a9","target":"COMP-f01e7a521b5470f4","source_occurrence_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R003::V::coordination.price_signal","target_occurrence_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R003::V::clearing.social_welfare","source_unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::coordination.price_signal","target_unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::clearing.social_welfare","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Deterministic, single-time-step exogenous P2P economic dispatch (5). | Endogenous, network-constrained P2P economic-dispatch reference formulation (1).","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e07","v141-b-g01-e14","v141-b-g03-e11","v141-b-g03-e15"]},"active":true,"source_signature":"910b7a3b010afb208ed5bd6ef90bb102ecd7e3aa006cc544a39e65c40f8f4581","relation_scope":"original_occurrence_pair_only","internal_to_parent":false,"source_science":{"mechanism_id":"v141-M01","source_definition":"An offered energy quantity in a partner's counterproposal supplies an input to the receiving agent's bilateral trading-price update. Scope: Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: For received counterproposals offering energy; the receiving agent has received all partner counterproposals. A requested energy quantity in a partner's counterproposal supplies an input to the receiving agent's bilateral trading-price update. Scope: Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: For received counterproposals requesting energy; the receiving agent has received all partner counterproposals. Network-charge signals are provided transparently before negotiations so participants can anticipate additional costs of using the power system. Scope: Exogenous network-charge signals in the P2P negotiation mechanism. Condition: When exogenous terms are used as network charges. Unit-fee signals may be adapted to grid status, including differences between day and night. Scope: Electrical-distance and zonal network-charge policies, whose underlying grid characteristics are assumed time independent. The system operator may update unit-fee signals based on the previous period's revenue adequacy and congestion occurrence rate; yearly revision is an example, not a mandatory frequency. Scope: Periodic revision of network-charge unit-fee signals under the proposed cost-allocation policies. Condition: When the system operator periodically revises unit fees using historical information from the previous period. Each agent broadcasts its local residuals and tests both global primal and dual stopping criteria; the decentralized negotiation repeats until convergence. Scope: Convergence checks in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: At each iteration, the global stopping test follows receipt of all other agents' local residuals.","target_definition":"Network-charge price incentives, adjusted through unit fees, influence the aggregate-cost-minimizing P2P economic dispatch. Scope: Deterministic, single-time-step exogenous P2P economic dispatch (5). Condition: When the proposed exogenous network-charge policies are applied. The reference clearing objective minimizes the sum of agents' cost functions. Scope: Endogenous, network-constrained P2P economic-dispatch reference formulation (1). Condition: For the reference formulation before replacement of network constraints by exogenous cost allocation.","constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Network-charge price incentives, adjusted through unit fees, influence the aggregate-cost-minimizing P2P economic dispatch. Scope: Deterministic, single-time-step exogenous P2P economic dispatch (5). Condition: When the proposed exogenous network-charge policies are applied.","expression":"\\min_{P,p_n\\in\\Omega,W}\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left[f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}\\right]","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","clearing.social_welfare"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R003-X001","v141-c-R003-X002","v141-c-R003-X003","v141-c-R003-X004"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R003-T001","v141-c-R003-T002","v141-c-R003-T003"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e14","v141-b-g03-e11","v141-b-g03-e15","v141-b-g01-e13"]}],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"for its use of the power system. In problem (3) with (4), reciprocity constraint (3b) is the of only","equation_id":"Eq. (5)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In problem (3) with (4), reciprocity constraint (3b) is the only barrier to fully distribute it. To overcome this an additional slack variable W can be considered. Variable W, which can contribute to reach consensus, aims at being the image of all possible trades P. For this, reciprocity constraint (3b) is replaced by power consensus constraint (5b) leading to the deterministic, single time-step, exogenous P2P economic dispatch \\min_{P,p_n\\in\\Omega,W}\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left[f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}\\right] (5a); s.t. (W-W^{T})/2=P (5b); p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}, n\\in\\Omega (5c); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega (5d); p_{nm}\\ge0, n\\in\\Omega_g (5e); p_{nm}\\le0, n\\in\\Omega_c (5f); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega_p. (5g)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p09-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Case Studies","anchor_text":"p y , y q allocation, electrical distance and zonal cost allocations enable ma to isolate zones econ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"Despite these results, market aggregation by zone does not reflect how the electrical grid is used. Cost allocation policies provides a tool for the system operator to affect the economic dispatch. Unit fees enable to tweak the expected outcomes of the P2P market in such way that line capacities might not be violated. They also offer to the system operator the chance to look for cost recovery."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e15","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p10-c2r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Case Studies","anchor_text":"nomic dispatch, the proposed method based on network charges brings transparency and is simple to im","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Peer-to-peer markets are considered as a likely evolution of the power systems driven by distributed energy resources and ICT development. In this paper a peer-to-peer electricity market including network charges has been considered. Network charges, provided a priori, have been used as incentives to account for grid-related costs in a simple and transparent way. This mechanism incites market participants to respect power system’s limits, rather than enforcing them. Tested for three incentive frameworks, on a novel test case based on the IEEE 39-bus test system, it has been shown the ability of this mechanism to limit the stress put on the physical grid by the market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e13","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"t s pu pose. e , egu a at o u ct o h eva uat g et 2) work constraints is be replaced by a cost alloc","equation_id":"Eq. (4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"However, even though (3) enables the system operator to recover congestion related costs, it does not guarantee the recovery of the costs of maintenance, modernization of power lines, taxes, and policies. This paper proposes to replace the regularization function h with exogenous terms. These exogenous terms would aim not only at allocating congestion-related costs but also costs of maintenance and modernization of power lines, taxes, and policies such as e.g. renewable support schemes. Preference prices as introduced in [18] seems a good candidate for this purpose. Then, regularization function h evaluating network constraints is be replaced by a cost allocation function defined as \\Gamma(\\mathbf{P})=\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left(\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}\\right) (4); where parameter \\gamma_{nm} is the network charge associated to power trade p_{nm} for the given time step. Constant terms \\gamma_n^0, which do not affect the minimization outcome, allow reflecting costs that are independent of the power traded, such as power line investment and maintenance. Network charges \\gamma_{nm}, detailed in Section III, would then account for congestion-related costs and taxes. Function \\Gamma is separable among participants, and will be integrated in their objective function as it will be further discussed in Section III. Note that \\Gamma also represents the amount of money collected by the system operator from community \\Omega for its use of the power system."}],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Deterministic, single-time-step exogenous P2P economic dispatch (5). | Endogenous, network-constrained P2P economic-dispatch reference formulation (1).","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e07","v141-b-g01-e14","v141-b-g03-e11","v141-b-g03-e15"]},"source_record":{"edge_id":"v141-c-R003-E001","source":"coordination.price_signal","target":"clearing.social_welfare","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R003-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e14","v141-b-g03-e11","v141-b-g03-e15"]},"paper_scope_note":"The bilateral negotiated price update and the exogenous network fee signal are separately limited in meaning; partner-set constraints, incentive intent, and ADMM computational dependencies cannot be transmitted in a mixed way."}});
